<title>YUGOSLAVIA: Yugoslav January-June tourism down 14.7 pct on '96.</title>
<headline>Yugoslav January-June tourism down 14.7 pct on '96.</headline>
<dateline>BELGRADE 1997-07-31</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Federal Statistics Office reported the following economic indicator details.</p>
<p> YUGOSLAV TOURISM	   Jan-June 97	Percent Change</p>
<p>						   (index base Jan-Jun 96)</p>
<p> Total tourists		1,160,322	     -14.7</p>
<p> Yugoslavs		     1,031,622	     -15.8</p>
<p> Ex-Yugoslav republics	  59,464	     - 7.0</p>
<p> Foreign			    69,236	     - 1.4</p>
<p> Overnights		    4,248,868	     -13.7</p>
<p>NOTES -- Most foreign visitors came from Italy (8,790), followed by the Russian Federation (6,249), Germany (5,726), Greece (5,173), Bulgaria (5,065) and Austria (3,893).</p>
<p>According to National Bank of Yugoslavia data, $16.9 million of hard currency income from tourism was registered in the January-June period, or 7.7 percent less than in the same six-month period last year.</p>
<p>Figures refer to the Federal Republic of Yugoslavia, comprising Serbia and Montenegro.</p>
<p>Ex-Yugoslav republics refer to Bosnia, Croatia, Macedonia and Slovenia.</p>
<p>-- Amra Kevic, Belgrade newsroom +381 11 311 4305</p>
